# The tools of the toolbox image, one row per fetched tool, in install order.
# Fields, separated by one space: <name> <version> <sha256> <address>
# The sha256 is the one the upstream release publishes for the file at the address. The rows of the database clients are the exception:
# their file is the signing key of the vendor's apt repository, for which no upstream checksum exists, so the sha256 is that of the key file
# as read at the address on the date below, and apt then holds every package to the signature of the repository.
# A line starting with # and an empty line are not rows.
# Every value was read at its source on 2026-10-31.
#
# node: newest LTS line (nodejs.org/dist/index.json); sha256 from https://nodejs.org/dist/v26.13.0/SHASUMS256.txt
node 26.13.0 5848553868b402d3fabb5b09270649977780a390f832155721025a08b5104455 https://nodejs.org/dist/v26.13.0/node-v26.13.0-linux-x64.tar.gz
# npm: dist-tag latest of the npm registry; sha256 of the registry tarball, which matches the sha512 integrity of https://registry.npmjs.org/npm/12.2.1
npm 12.2.1 228bd64aaef23270dbdbd2cf3d5ef5c467602ef38c4066246cef1984adab9a67 https://registry.npmjs.org/npm/-/npm-12.2.1.tgz
# pnpm: dist-tag latest of the npm registry. The standalone release file, not the npm tarball: the tarball fetches its native binary from the
# registry when it first runs, which a pod cannot do. sha256 is the digest GitHub states for the release asset (gh api repos/pnpm/pnpm/releases/tags/v12.16.1);
# its pnpm binary is byte-identical to the one in @pnpm/exe.linux-x64 12.16.1, whose sha512 integrity the npm registry states
pnpm 12.16.1 565ff81174a14f6f7bd5d84c1b17e51031a80230d370a1fe24be5d16bd02a825 https://github.com/pnpm/pnpm/releases/download/v12.16.1/pnpm-linux-x64.tar.gz
# dart: stable channel (VERSION file of the stable channel); sha256 from the .sha256sum beside the file in the dart-archive
dart 3.13.7 eb99aed266efbcb615c6ae21c12f52cfe935b9f208bccc5fea7f64c21e867229 https://storage.googleapis.com/dart-archive/channels/stable/release/3.13.7/sdk/dartsdk-linux-x64-release.zip
# helm: newest release; sha256 from https://get.helm.sh/helm-v4.3.1-linux-amd64.tar.gz.sha256sum
helm v4.3.1 0403a88ae20913d11b10a2467b50087721fea7581736da6b9d3992f5357fe54b https://get.helm.sh/helm-v4.3.1-linux-amd64.tar.gz
# yq: newest release; sha256 is the SHA-256 column of the release file "checksums", ordered by its file "checksums_hashes_order"
yq v4.54.1 8e34fc298390875de416e6a4afcb8cabeceb25d9aa8506c1a2f9353cf702ea5f https://github.com/mikefarah/yq/releases/download/v4.54.1/yq_linux_amd64
# promtool: NOT THE NEWEST RELEASE ON PURPOSE (3.15.1 is out). It is the Prometheus that kube-prometheus-stack 92.13.1 runs, and promtool
# validates rules against the version that runs, so it moves with that chart. sha256 from the release file sha256sums.txt
promtool 3.15.0 2a542df32eac02ee17b9d844fb2aa1de00dafa5476579ba8a3ba862e9d572ea0 https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v3.15.0/prometheus-3.15.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
# gitleaks: NOT THE NEWEST RELEASE ON PURPOSE (8.30.1 is out). Newer releases report a line in a test that a consumer's release scan passes
# today, so it moves with that scan. sha256 from the release file gitleaks_8.21.2_checksums.txt
gitleaks v8.21.2 5bc41815076e6ed6ef8fbecc9d9b75bcae31f39029ceb55da08086315316e3ba https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v8.21.2/gitleaks_8.21.2_linux_x64.tar.gz
# powershell: newest release; sha256 from the release file hashes.sha256
powershell 7.6.9 1da6d6d6035d651f20e70ddee7c4c15ac6ab8b9fb40ee92240d91263f28a884b https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/releases/download/v7.6.9/powershell-7.6.9-linux-x64.tar.gz
# kubectl: the newest patch of the 1.35 line (https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable-1.35.txt), the minor of the Kubernetes the jobs' clusters run.
# kubectl is within one minor of its API server either way, and the row moves with the clusters' minor. sha256 from the .sha256 file beside the binary
kubectl v1.35.10 c49a28f1f4efc4d475ed05f47f2ce8e563bf7bef69ed77fe1b924ea6d83b6327 https://dl.k8s.io/release/v1.35.10/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl
# kubeconform: NOT THE NEWEST RELEASE ON PURPOSE (0.8.0 is out). 0.6.7 is the release the manifest checks that move onto this image run today, so
# each check keeps its version while it moves. sha256 from the release file CHECKSUMS
kubeconform v0.6.7 95f14e87aa28c09d5941f11bd024c1d02fdc0303ccaa23f61cef67bc92619d73 https://github.com/yannh/kubeconform/releases/download/v0.6.7/kubeconform-linux-amd64.tar.gz
# rclone: newest release (https://downloads.rclone.org/version.txt). sha256 from the release file SHA256SUMS, whose PGP signature by the rclone
# release key (https://rclone.org/KEYS, fingerprint FBF737ECE9F8AB18604BD2AC93935E02FF3B54FA) was checked
rclone v1.75.2 349ac8fba6ff65d6247043f1750cdcb518ec5d500ef91463a10d37c0ccdf3702 https://github.com/rclone/rclone/releases/download/v1.75.2/rclone-v1.75.2-linux-amd64.zip
# The database clients come from the vendors' apt repositories on the base's Debian suite, because Debian's own archive carries one frozen
# series of each. The version of a row is the series of the database server the client dumps and restores, and the build installs the newest
# package of that series; test.sh holds the printed version to the series. Where the series says nothing about the client's own number, the row is
# the exact version. A server that leaves its series needs a new toolbox.
# mongodump and mongosh: MongoDB's apt repository of the 8.0 series, named by the address of its signing key. The database tools (100.x) and mongosh (2.x)
# are numbered apart from the server, and the repositories of the series 7.0 and 8.0 carry the same builds, so the row is the exact version, the newest
# of the 8.0 repository for trixie (https://repo.mongodb.org/apt/debian/dists/trixie/mongodb-org/8.0/main/binary-amd64/Packages)
mongodump 100.20.0 8c467ea138207ee8d0cbbce3e005c82c73c81b3263e7909bdc36b9a1feff14f6 https://pgp.mongodb.com/server-8.0.asc
mongosh 2.14.1 8c467ea138207ee8d0cbbce3e005c82c73c81b3263e7909bdc36b9a1feff14f6 https://pgp.mongodb.com/server-8.0.asc
# pg_dumpall (with psql): package postgresql-client-18 of the PostgreSQL project's apt repository, https://apt.postgresql.org/pub/repos/apt trixie-pgdg
pg_dumpall 18 0144068502a1eddd2a0280ede10ef607d1ec592ce819940991203941564e8e76 https://www.postgresql.org/media/keys/ACCC4CF8.asc
# redis-cli (package redis-tools) and redis-server: Redis' apt repository, https://packages.redis.io/deb trixie, which carries every series, so the
# build pins the packages to the series. A restore starts a throwaway redis-server from the dump
redis-cli 8.8 817b5a78358d00ed6b71884d70ad5d2eab9934badca1a34299fdc6a2e4a8ad20 https://packages.redis.io/gpg
redis-server 8.8 817b5a78358d00ed6b71884d70ad5d2eab9934badca1a34299fdc6a2e4a8ad20 https://packages.redis.io/gpg
# mariadb (with mariadb-dump): package mariadb-client of MariaDB's apt repository of the 12.3 series,
# https://dlm.mariadb.com/repo/mariadb-server/12.3/repo/debian trixie
mariadb 12.3 403e0403df51273fbf5613e5644959d3f85923ef7d8f92e7d83eafe289093af4 https://supplychain.mariadb.com/mariadb-keyring-2025.gpg
